---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'About — Guy Friley / 0xguy';
const description = 'Who Guy is, how he got here, and what he is working on now.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>

  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Guy Friley</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      Pittsburgh, PA. Technical investigator by day, homelab tinkerer by evening, grad student by weekend.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <section>
    <h2>What I do</h2>
    <p>
      For the past four years I've been a <strong>technical investigator</strong> on an enterprise platform — resolving complex issues across 15+ products, mostly in the nonprofit and education verticals.
      The work spans Apex source-level debugging, SOQL/Tooling API queries, correlating telemetry in Splunk (corepods, gack traces, memory profiling), and building reproduction harnesses to make intermittent defects reproducible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Over that stretch I've closed <strong>1,687 enterprise cases</strong> with a 4.9/5.0 median CSAT and 0% escalation rate since 2025, opened <strong>106 Structured Investigations</strong> that produced 64 engineering work items (60% escalation-to-code-fix rate), authored 22 Knowledge Articles, filed 7 Known Issues, and led 58 Swarm assists.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2025 I was recognized as an <em>Agentblazer Champion &amp; Innovator</em> for building AI-powered service agent solutions using Agentforce, Einstein AI, Data Cloud, and Prompt Builder.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I'm working on</h2>
    <p>
      I've spent four years at the application layer. I'm now exploring the layers underneath — hypervisors, networks, pipelines, security controls — on my own hardware, in public.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Building out a <strong>homelab</strong> — Proxmox 9.1 + Tailscale + a growing roster of self-hosted services</li>
      <li>Certification roadmap: <strong>Security+ → Network+ → CCNA</strong>, on top of the TryHackMe Pre Security path</li>
      <li>Deep-diving Docker, with <strong>Kubernetes</strong> next</li>
      <li>Finishing a <strong>Master of Science in Data Science</strong> at the University of Pittsburgh (2026)</li>
      <li>Writing everything up at <a href="https://publish.obsidian.md/0xguy/Home" class="ext-link">publish.obsidian.md/0xguy</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I'm genuinely uncertain where this lands. Could be platform engineering, could be SRE, could be something cloud/architecture-adjacent I haven't fully scoped. The skills compound either way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p>
      Not a linear path.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2015–2019</strong> — Home Theater Specialist at Best Buy. Learned to explain technical things to non-technical humans, train junior staff, and survive a retail floor.</li>
      <li><strong>2019</strong> — Salesforce Business Analyst (contract) at World-Class Industrial Network. First exposure to Salesforce.</li>
      <li><strong>2019–2020</strong> — IT Infrastructure Rep at Highmark Health, supporting Allegheny Health Network hospital staff. First real enterprise IT; first Sev 1 response.</li>
      <li><strong>2020–2021</strong> — Salesforce Developer / Admin at Virtual Clarity (DXC). Built the firm's first automated Azure AD → Salesforce provisioning pipeline.</li>
      <li><strong>2021–present</strong> — Technical investigator role. Current chapter.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I like working on</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Finding root causes.</strong> The gap between <em>"it works again"</em> and <em>"here's exactly why it broke"</em> is the gap between patching and engineering.</li>
      <li><strong>Making systems legible.</strong> Good documentation, clear diagrams, and honest postmortems are undervalued.</li>
      <li><strong>Shipping small, finished things.</strong> Finished beats ambitious-but-half-built every time.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
